schema: claim-evidence
object:
  title: Bristol Pound and Bristol Pay wound down for lack of funding, not a flawed idea
  type: claim-evidence
  claim: >-
    The Bristol Pound ran a respected local currency in the UK for nearly a
    decade and still wound down in 2020-2021, not because the idea was
    wrong, but because the organization couldn't secure the funding to keep
    the lights on. Its successor, Bristol Pay, tried to carry the mission
    forward in digital form and was itself discontinued in 2023, again for
    lack of funding.
  evidence: >-
    Cited in the article's References to "Bristol Pound (Wikipedia)"
    (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bristol_pound).
  source_lineage: src/content/docs/minimum-viable-node.md
  interpretation: >-
    Used in the article as a sustainability cautionary tale — the takeaway
    given is "design for sustainability from week one," not "don't bother."
  uncertainty: >-
    Sourced to a single tertiary reference (Wikipedia); dates and cause of
    discontinuation not independently cross-checked against primary sources
    for this ingestion.
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/minimum-viable-node.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted
